    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d981">

                <pb facs="1352" n="1340" sameAs="frus:frus1945v06/pg1340"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">741.92/9–2645: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-09-27T18:00:00-04:00">September 27,
                            1945—6 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8550. Urtel 9988 Sep 26. Dept appreciates assurance by Sterndale Bennett that
                    there was no intention implicate US in Brit-Siamese agreement and that further
                    modifications to meet US views under consideration. Brit Embassy informed Dept
                    Sep 26 that Dening himself notified Siamese that US had some objections to
                    agreement. This eliminated Dept concern that Siamese would believe US supported
                    agreement. US has no desire to interfere in bilateral Brit-Siamese negotiations.
                    Yost has said nothing which might prejudice negotiations <pb facs="1353"
                        n="1341" xml:id="pg1341"/> and Dept has expressly instructed him<anchor
                        n="74" xml:id="d981-f1"/> not to convey to Siamese US views on any points or
                    details of agreement or military annex.</p>
                <p>Please inform Bennett of foregoing. At same time it might be well to explain that
                    on short term conditions to settlement of war US after offering its views
                    expressly recognized that those conditions were of Brit-Siamese concern only;
                    but that proposed agreement is one of final war settlements and US is concerned
                    in long range aspects of agreement such as Clause 1 of Section C and Clause 4 of
                    Section D. For your information Dept is especially anxious that Brit meet US
                    views on latter clause.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
